# POSITIVE (RV-A over-rejection guard): a NON-GENERIC user struct named `Guard`
# with a REAL field must typecheck and run normally — in SEMANTIC ANALYSIS the
# wrapper flag is a typed field on the builtin DefIds only, so sharing the name
# does not trip the field-access reject.
#
# ⚠ SCOPE — ONE CELL, AND THE OTHER CELL ON THIS AXIS IS BROKEN. This samples
# only the NON-GENERIC spelling, whose mangled type name is bare `Guard` with no
# `__` suffix; that is the entire reason it passes. LOWERING does not read the
# typed flag — it decides "is this a guard?" by `strip_prefix("Guard__")` on the
# MANGLED name — so the GENERIC spelling `struct Guard[T]` mangles to
# `Guard__int64_t`, is typedef'd to the runtime `gorget_guard_t`, and fails the
# C build. That cell is pinned by the `#[ignore]`d
# `known_gaps/fieldaccess_user_generic_guard_collision.gg`.
#
# So this fixture does NOT show that "sharing a builtin wrapper's name costs the
# user nothing" — it shows that the non-generic spelling is free. Do not widen
# the claim without adding the generic cell.
struct Guard:
    int x

int main():
    Guard g = Guard(3)
    print(g.x)
    return 0
